282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Cleveland music people reported an average household income of $82K. Of this income, 26% or an average annual income of $21K, is earned from music work. Across all respondents, music makers generate a total of $58 million dollars in income annually. Yet, the workforce is still primarily freelance and independent, with 65% of respondents working another job outside of music. Only 17% of respondents have received any form of financial assistance, so a range of grants, tax breaks, or incentives could further increase the local economy of the Cleveland music ecosystem.)
/E (Cleveland music people reported an average household income of $82K. Of this income, 26% or an average annual income of $21K, is earned from music work. Across all respondents, music makers generate a total of $58 million dollars in income annually. Yet, the workforce is still primarily freelance and independent, with 65% of respondents working another job outside of music. Only 17% of respondents have received any form of financial assistance, so a range of grants, tax breaks, or incentives could further increase the local economy of the Cleveland music ecosystem.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Cleveland respondents find it very livable, noting some affordability issues. A super majority plan to keep working in music and stay in Cleveland. In terms of culture and belonging, the Cleveland music ecosystem is somewhat challenged by elitism, ageism, and ableism, but on the positive side, a majority of respondents experience the Cleveland music ecosystem as safe, diverse, and inclusive. Cleveland would do well to enhance efforts to support underrepresented groups and foster a collaborative environment, reducing competitiveness and exclusivity. Respondents want to connect with other creative industries, with music services, and to collaborate musically. )
/E (Cleveland respondents find it very livable, noting some affordability issues. A super majority plan to keep working in music and stay in Cleveland. In terms of culture and belonging, the Cleveland music ecosystem is somewhat challenged by elitism, ageism, and ableism, but on the positive side, a majority of respondents experience the Cleveland music ecosystem as safe, diverse, and inclusive. Cleveland would do well to enhance efforts to support underrepresented groups and foster a collaborative environment, reducing competitiveness and exclusivity. Respondents want to connect with other creative industries, with music services, and to collaborate musically. )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
